  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Advocacy and communication with communities to empower for accessing social services (ACCESS)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project targets marginalised rural poor people, particularly women, people with disabilities, adolescents and children from extremely poor families, those without land or assets, and fishermen&#x2019;s families who are denied basic services.  It works by establishing ganokendras (GK), a kind of community-based organisation which links up grassroots-level communities with Community Resource Centres (CRCs) established at union level.  (A union, meaning a group of villages, is the lowest tier of local government in Bangladesh.)  The GKs and CRCs promote basic literacy and help people to be aware of, and to access, government services to which they are entitled: these include health and education services, support with livelihoods development, and social security for the most vulnerable.   The project also provides skills training and microcredit to help people earn a better income, and vocational training for young people which includes use of computers and the internet.  Communities are mobilised to analyse the risks they face from natural disasters (which are very common in Bangladesh) and to improve their capacity to cope with emergencies as they arise.</narrative>
  </description>
